.\" Manpage for hostnamectl (hostnamed bash client)
.TH HOSTNAMECTL 1 "October 2026" "hostnamed 0.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hostnamectl \- control the system hostname and related machine metadata
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hostnamectl
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR COMMAND " [" ARGUMENT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hostnamectl
is a Bash client used to query and change the system's static, transient,
and pretty hostnames, along with related machine metadata such as the
icon name, chassis type, deployment environment, and location.
.PP
Changes are sent to the system D-Bus service
.BR hostnamed (8)
through the
.B org.freedesktop.hostname1
interface. The daemon validates requests, authorizes privileged changes
through PolKit, updates the running hostname where appropriate, and writes
the persistent configuration files. The client does not write to
.I /etc/HOSTNAME
(or
.IR /etc/hostname )
or
.IR /etc/machine-info
directly. A caller does not need to be root when PolKit authorizes the
requested operation; authorization failures are reported by the D-Bus call.
.PP
The
.B status
command currently reads the configuration files and system information
to format its human-readable output. Changes made through D-Bus are handled
by the daemon and reflected in its D-Bus properties.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show the current hostname and machine information in human-readable form.
This is the default command when none is given. Fields are only printed
when a value is available; for example the pretty hostname, deployment,
location, firmware fields, and virtualization line are omitted entirely
if empty or undetectable. The transient hostname is only shown when it
differs from the static hostname.
.TP
.BI set-hostname " [--static|--transient|--pretty] NAME"
Set the system hostname. With no mode flag, the static hostname is updated through
.BR hostnamed (8),
which also updates the running kernel hostname. The pretty hostname is
not changed implicitly. Static and transient hostnames are validated as
RFC 1123 hostnames, including label-length and hyphen-placement rules.
.RS
.TP
.B --static
Update the persistent static hostname through the daemon. The daemon also
updates the running hostname to match.
.TP
.B --transient
Update only the running kernel's transient hostname through D-Bus,
without changing the static hostname file.
.TP
.B --pretty
Update only the pretty hostname in
.IR /etc/machine-info .
Pretty names are free-form and may contain spaces. A valid hostname
containing a domain suffix may be reduced to its first label; arbitrary
free-form dotted names are preserved.
.RE
.TP
.BI set-icon-name " NAME"
Set the freedesktop icon name (for example
.BR computer-laptop )
used to represent this machine in graphical tools. Stored as
.B ICON_NAME
in
.IR /etc/machine-info .
.TP
.BI set-chassis " TYPE"
Set the chassis type. Must be one of:
.BR desktop ", " laptop ", " server ", " tablet ", " handset ", "
.BR watch ", " embedded ", " vm ", " container ", or " convertible .
Stored as
.B CHASSIS
in
.IR /etc/machine-info .
.TP
.BI set-deployment " ENVIRONMENT"
Set a free-form deployment label, for example
.BR production " or " staging .
Stored as
.B DEPLOYMENT
in
.IR /etc/machine-info .
.TP
.BI set-location " LOCATION"
Set a free-form physical location string. Stored as
.B LOCATION
in
.IR /etc/machine-info .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show a short usage summary and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Show the current status:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hostnamectl status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Rename the machine entirely (static, transient, and pretty hostnames):
.PP
.RS
.EX
hostnamectl set-hostname web01.example.net
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give the machine a friendly display name without changing its network
hostname:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hostnamectl set-hostname --pretty "Nathan's Desktop"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Change only the running kernel's hostname, leaving the configured static
hostname untouched until the next reboot:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hostnamectl set-hostname --transient temphost
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mark a machine as part of staging:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hostnamectl set-deployment staging
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and 1 on error (for example, an invalid hostname,
an invalid chassis type, a missing argument, or insufficient
permissions).
.SH AUTHORIZATION
Change commands call the system D-Bus service
.B org.freedesktop.hostname1
and are authorized by the daemon's PolKit policy. Root and members of the
configured privileged group (by default
.BR wheel )
may be authorized without an authentication prompt, depending on the
installed PolKit rules. Other callers may be prompted or denied according
to policy. The
.B interactive
argument is enabled by this client so an authentication agent can be used
when policy requires it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/HOSTNAME
Static hostname file, used in preference to
.I /etc/hostname
when present.
.TP
.I /etc/hostname
Fallback static hostname file, used only if
.I /etc/HOSTNAME
does not exist.
.TP
.I /etc/machine-info
Stores the pretty hostname, icon name, chassis, deployment, and location
as shell-style
.B KEY="value"
assignments. The daemon updates this file atomically and preserves unrelated
keys and comments.
.TP
.I /etc/os-release
Read for the operating system's pretty name and CPE name when displaying
.BR status .
Not modified by
.BR hostnamectl .
.TP
.I /etc/machine-id
Read for the machine ID shown in
.BR status .
Not modified by
.BR hostnamectl .
.TP
.I /sys/class/dmi/id/*
SMBIOS/DMI sysfs nodes read for chassis detection, virtualization
detection, and the hardware vendor, model, firmware version, and
firmware date fields shown in
.BR status .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hostnamed (8),
.BR machine-info (5),
.BR os-release (5),
.BR hostname (1),
 .BR inotify (7)
.SH AUTHOR
Written by Nathaniel Russell.
.PP
Part of the
.B hostnamed
project, a non-systemd implementation of the
.B org.freedesktop.hostname1
D-Bus interface for Slackware Linux.
